hi i'm evan carmichael and today i  wanted to talk about a topic  that a lot of entrepreneurs are scared  about but shouldn't be  and that's your balance sheet many  entrepreneurs  no balance sheet is something their  accountant gives them at the end of the  year  but they don't really know how to use it  to make important business decisions  so in this video i wanted to quickly  talk about what a balance sheet is what  the elements are  and then give you some tips and  strategies on how you can use the  information in your balance sheet  to run your business potentially raise  money for your business and also  sell your business here we go  to quickly explain what a balance sheet  is i've loaded my simply accounting  enterprise software  and you can go to reports click on  financials  over to balance sheet and this will help  me prepare a balance sheet for the  company  this is a construction company here and  there is the balance sheet  so the balance sheet will basically tell  you what your business  owns what it owes to other people  and then what you as a business owner  actually have  in the business that's left over so if  you look at the  assets here assets is what your company  will own  the liabilities are going to be what you  owe to other people  and then the equity is going to be what  you as the entrepreneur will own  in the actual business so the formula is  always  assets equals liabilities plus  equity or if you think about another way  the assets what your company  owns minus the liabilities what you owe  is  what you end up with as your equity in  in the company and hopefully  that's a lot of money so i want to  quickly just touch base on what each  category is so you have a  understanding under assets  you can look at the first category here  is total current assets  and this these are assets that are  either cash or could be converted into  cash really quickly  so you can see here cash is here and  then usually the biggest one in the  current assets are receivables  so accounts receivables are money that's  basically owed to you  now you've you've either invoiced them  and they're you're waiting on payment  could be 30 days or 60 days or 90 days  and you're waiting to get paid so  hopefully that'll be converted pretty  quickly  inventory assets is the second category  and these are items that you have that  hopefully can be converted quickly into  cash  if you need it so if we look at what  they have here  we've got drywall hardware lumber  roofing other  so it's a construction company that  would make sense if they really needed  to  generate cash they could probably sell  this and and make some money from it  the next category are capital assets and  these are items that you cannot convert  into cash  very easily but are still worth a lot of  money so you can see  on this balance sheet here that they've  got a lot of capital assets  so if you look at that you'll see things  like furniture  equipment vehicles computers building  land  so all things that have value but it's  hard to convert into money if they  really need to do it quickly  and then the last category are other  non-current assets  and these are things like goodwill or if  you have a patent that's potentially  worth a lot of money  you would filter it here under  non-current assets  the next side is liabilities and so this  is going to be what you owe  as a company again it's going to be  divided into current liabilities and  long-term liabilities  so for current liabilities this is money  that you basically will owe  within the next year so it could be  accounts payable  so something that you've bought that you  haven't paid off yet credit card debt  uh bank loans bank advances corporate  taxes  basically things that you need to pay  off within the next year  long-term liabilities uh is money that  you owe to other  people or other companies but that  aren't due until beyond a year  so it could be a bank loan or a mortgage  or a loan from a shareholder  things that you have to pay off but you  don't have to pay off within the next  year  so that's how much liabilities you'll  have and then the  remainder is going to be the equity and  that's going to be how much you will own  as the entrepreneur in the business so  again the key thing to look at  assets is what you own your company owns  liabilities  what your company owes to other people  or companies and if you subtract those  two you'll end up with the equity which  is how much you have actually left in  the business  so that's what a balance sheet is let me  now  show you how you can use the information  in your balance sheet  to run your business raise capital for  your business  and then potentially sell your business  as well  two important numbers that we can get  from our balance sheet to help us run  our businesses  are working capital and turnover in  accounts receivable  so we'll start with working capital  working capital you may have heard  before  it is basically your current assets  minus your current liabilities  so if we look at our current assets it's  350  000 almost minus our current liabilities  of two hundred and twenty thousand  dollars  so we've got some some good working  capital of about a hundred and thirty  thousand dollars what that basically  means is  this company can survive in the short  term so  if creditors come and say hey we need  our money now or if there's a  crunch that you know sales dry up and  the company needs to pay off  you know those liabilities they have the  money to do it  if you owe a lot of money in your  liabilities and you don't have money  coming in  if there's a big crunch if sales drop  off then it's going to be hard for you  to  raise the money to be able to pay people  off also being able to pay things like  salaries and day-to-day expenses are a  lot easier if you have  good working capital so you want to make  sure that your current assets  are greater than your current  liabilities and that'll help  ensure that your company can survive in  the short term  the second thing to look at is your  accounts receivable  and a big part of your current assets  here we could see  is our accounts receivable so for this  company you know we have 346 000 current  assets  accounts receivable is a big chunk of  that 236 000.  this could mean i'm getting money right  away or could mean  i'm not getting money for a long time so  you want to look at  how frequently that money comes in so  is it you know 30 days payable is it 60  days payable is it 90 days payable  because if you have a lot of liabilities  if you owe people money that's coming up  you know the bank needs their money  regardless then  you have to make sure that that money is  going to come in on time  the other thing you want to make sure of  is that people actually pay your  accounts receivable  and that you're working with companies  who will pay  on time so companies that you know don't  go bankrupt or refuse to pay you  you're still showing it here as hey you  know i'm over two hundred thousand  dollars  but if they don't actually end up paying  you the money then it's going to be hard  time for you to be able to run your  business  so those two key numbers are really  important to look at for growing your  business  again you want to look at your working  capital so your current assets minus  your current liabilities  and that'll tell you if you're able to  survive in the short term with your  business  and then look at your accounts  receivable and see when they're due  if the money is going to be coming in on  time for you to pay off what you owe  other companies and if it's likely that  that money will come in based on the  types of clientele that you're working  with  next up is raising capital if you're  trying to raise capital  from bankers or from investors they want  to definitely take a look at your  balance sheet  so you can make it as strong as you can  by first making sure that there's assets  to lend against so they won't care so  much about your current assets but they  want to look at your  capital assets to see if there's  anything that they can  lend against so especially bankers want  to see that you have a lot of  assets so if you have land or if you  have a building  or if you have any furniture equipment  that has some significant  value then they're thinking well if they  don't pay off this debt  i can take away that building i could  take away that land and sell it and i  can make my money back  they want to have that security that you  have an asset there that they can take  away from you if you don't make your  payments  so they want to make sure that you have  those capital assets available  the other thing that they're going to  want to look at is how much debt do you  already have  so they're going to look at your  liabilities especially the long-term  liabilities  and see do you already have bank loans  do you already have a mortgage you have  loans from shareholders  if you already have a lot of liabilities  and you're owing people a lot of money  it's going to be a lot harder for you  you to be able to raise capital because  even though you have great assets  you're already maxed out and it's harder  to leverage those assets even more  if you're trying to sell your business  the potential buyer is definitely going  to want to look at your balance sheet  so i want to talk about a couple things  you're going to want to look at  if we're looking at assets they're going  to definitely check out your current  assets and look at your receivables  so your accounts receivables they're  going to want to see how big is it  and do your clients have a history of  pain like yes well we have 236 000  in accounts receivable but you know our  clients don't often pay us on time or  don't pay us at all  then you're going to get that number  knocked back significantly  the other thing here is inventory assets  if you carry a lot of inventory  they're going to see what is the  likelihood that you're actually going to  use  all of this stuff in your business and  if they had to sell it for cash if  something happened and you had to get  rid of  you know your hardware for example could  you actually sell it for 98  000 is it worth that much or is it worth  a lot less if you're gonna sell  the actual hardware itself so they're  going to look at  every single number on your balance  sheet and start asking your question to  see if your company is actually worth  the amount that you say it's worth one  last thing to look at here  we had the other non-current assets this  again is patents goodwills  here it's also called intangible assets  they're called intangible because it's  really hard to get an actual value on it  how do you value what a patent is worth  so you might think it's worth a lot  often buyers will say you know zero  dollars i'm not giving any value  assigned to that so something to be  prepared for  the other thing if i just close this up  that a potential investor is going to  look at  is do you have a track record of success  so you can create a balance sheet for  every year that you've been in business  and hopefully you've been around for  a little bit and they're going to want  to see that you've grown the business so  that you've  you've gained assets and that you've  gained in your  equity so they can see that your  business is growing you're acquiring  more assets  it could be land and buildings and that  kind of stuff more equipment to grow the  business  and also it's great that you've acquired  more assets but  they want to see that you're reducing  your liabilities or at least your equity  is growing  faster than your liabilities so if your  assets are going up  then either your liabilities or your  equity have to be going up  so hopefully it's the equity side and  that's what your your own value in the  business is  instead of you financing the growth by  taking on tons of debt  a new a new owner doesn't want to come  into a business and have to pay off huge  amounts of debt  so they're typically going to want to  look at three years or five years growth  if you have that  if you don't have that then as much as  you can but definitely be prepared with  your balance sheets because it makes you  look a lot more professional  makes you look like a serious business  person and it increases the chances that  you'll be able to sell your business and  hopefully get a good price for it  thank you for joining me for this video  i hope you enjoyed and it gave you some  more information about what a balance  sheet is  and how you can use it to make important  decisions for your business  if you did enjoy the video please give  it a thumbs up below  i'd also love to hear your thoughts and  feedback if you want to leave a comment  and stay tuned for the next episode  you

